        },
        {
            "block_label": "text",
            "block_content": "But if you create too many short punctuated sentences, your readers may feel your prose is choppy or simplistic. Experienced writers often revise very short grammatical sentences into subordinate clauses or even phrases, turning two or more grammatical sentences into one. Here, the subordinating conjunction when creates a subordinate clause (underlined) that appears before the subject of the main sentence:",
